      "text": "1. Timothy's quotient will be an integer if and only if Josef picks a divisor of 1000.\n2. To find the number of divisors of 1000, we need to prime factorize 1000.\n3. $1000 = 2^3 \\cdot 5^3$.\n4. The formula for finding the number of divisors of a number $n = p_1^{e_1} \\cdot p_2^{e_2} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot p_k^{e_k}$ is $(e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot (e_k + 1)$.\n   \\[\n   (3 + 1)(3 + 1) = 4 \\cdot 4 = 16\n   \\]\n5. Since we are only considering divisors between 1 and 1000 inclusive, we need to find the number of divisors of 1000 within this range.\n6. We need to check if each of the 16 divisors is within the range of 1 to 1000.\n7. Note that 1 and 1000 are divisors of 1000 and are within the range.\n8. We can manually check the divisors to find out which ones are between 1 and 1000. The divisors of 1000 between 1 and 1000 are:\n   \\[\n   1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 25, 40, 50, 100, 125, 200, 250, 500, 1000\n   \\]\n9. Counting these divisors, we get 14 divisors that are between 1 and 1000.\n10. Therefore, the number of integers Josef could pick such that Timothy's quotient is an integer is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
